# diary's settings (linked to ~/.system/config/diary/config). One setting a
# line: a name, a space, the value. Lines starting with # are comments.
# Left out, a setting has the value shown.

# the diary file
#file ~/.diary

# the editor in the right pane, with its options. When it isn't there,
# diary takes nvim, else vim, else vi.
#editor nvim

# private yes: the editor runs with -i NONE, so it keeps no history of
# what you typed, searched or yanked in the diary (shada/viminfo), and
# its undo files, views, backups and swap files go into RAM next to
# the diary, not onto the disk. They work as set while the diary is
# open, and are wiped when it closes.
#private yes

# programs for o (open an attachment): the endings, then the program
# and its options. Text (.txt, .md) opens in the editor. Once any
# viewer line is here, only the ones here count.
#viewer jpg,jpeg,png,gif,webp,bmp,tif,tiff,svg,heic,avif feh
#viewer pdf,epub,djvu,cbz,mobi sioyek
#viewer mp4,mkv,webm,mov,avi,mp3,ogg,opus,flac,wav,m4a,aac mpv

# the program that takes the metadata out of attachments
#clean clean

# the shell for ! (else $SHELL)
#shell ksh

# utf8 no: show only ASCII; other bytes become ?
#utf8 yes
